# The catalogue: the theories of the standard library

The library is sha256:292894befd300e536cdd1181db772f44fe3f3f7ad6ff52b1467f1735ff83d681. A program selects a theory by importing its module, listing after :symbols every short name of the module the program writes, and writing the facts that select the theory over its own relations, exactly as the entry writes them. A name the program writes and the import does not list is the program's own, and a fact of it selects nothing, in silence. Each entry is a program the kernel ran, with its answer.

## std.algebra: properties of a relation of two places, each selected by one fact of its name; the import lists every name the program writes

### (transitive R)

R is transitive: R a b and R b c give R a c. Its rule's own words: "{?rel} is transitive."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing.
The kernel's answer: Ann is an ancestor of Bob. Ann is an ancestor of Cy.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (transitive))

(relation ancestor-of Person Person :why "a {?3} is an ancestor of a {?4}")
(ancestor-of Ann Bob)
(ancestor-of Bob Cy)

;; R a b and R b c give R a c.
(transitive ancestor-of)

(query :goal (ancestor-of Ann ?d) :goal-text "Ann is an ancestor of {?d}.")
(solve :no-search)
```

### (symmetric R)

R is symmetric: R a b gives R b a, and a stored negation is mirrored too. Its rule's own words: "{?rel} is symmetric: {?a} ↔ {?b}."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing.
The kernel's answer: Bob is married to Ann.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (symmetric))

(relation is-married-to Person Person :why "a {?3} is married to a {?4}")
(is-married-to Ann Bob)

;; R a b gives R b a.
(symmetric is-married-to)

(query :goal (is-married-to Bob ?w) :goal-text "Bob is married to {?w}.")
(solve :no-search)
```

### (includes R S)

S includes R: every fact of R is a fact of S. Its rule's own words: "{?q} includes {?p}: from {?a} →{?p}→ {?b}, derive {?a} →{?q}→ {?b}."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing.
The kernel's answer: Ann is an ancestor of Bob.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (includes))

(relation is-parent-of   Person Person :why "a {?3} is a parent of a {?4}")
(relation is-ancestor-of Person Person :why "a {?3} is an ancestor of a {?4}")
(is-parent-of Ann Bob)

;; Every fact of R is a fact of S.
(includes is-parent-of is-ancestor-of)

(query :goal (is-ancestor-of ?a Bob) :goal-text "{?a} is an ancestor of Bob.")
(solve :no-search)
```

### (converse R S)

S is R turned round: R a b gives S b a, and S b a gives R a b. Its rule's own words: "(converse {?R1} {?R2}): mirror {?R1}({?a},{?b}) → {?R2}({?b},{?a})"
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing.
The kernel's answer: Bob is a child of Ann.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (converse))

(relation is-parent-of Person Person :why "a {?3} is a parent of a {?4}")
(relation is-child-of  Person Person :why "a {?3} is a child of a {?4}")
(is-parent-of Ann Bob)

;; S is R turned round: R a b gives S b a, and S b a gives R a b.
(converse is-parent-of is-child-of)

(query :goal (is-child-of Bob ?p) :goal-text "Bob is a child of {?p}.")
(solve :no-search)
```

### (compose R S T)

R then S is in T: R a b and S b c give T a c. Its rule's own words: "(compose {?R1} {?R2} {?R3}): {?R1}({?a},{?b})∧{?R2}({?b},{?c}) → {?R3}({?a},{?c})."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing.
The kernel's answer: Ann is a grandparent of Cy.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (compose))

(relation is-parent-of      Person Person :why "a {?3} is a parent of a {?4}")
(relation is-grandparent-of Person Person :why "a {?3} is a grandparent of a {?4}")
(is-parent-of Ann Bob)
(is-parent-of Bob Cy)

;; R then S is T: R a b and S b c give T a c.
(compose is-parent-of is-parent-of is-grandparent-of)

(query :goal (is-grandparent-of ?g Cy) :goal-text "{?g} is a grandparent of Cy.")
(solve :no-search)
```

### (top R isa Dom Ran)

R holds between every member of Dom and every member of Ran, a member being what the membership relation isa says. Its rule's own words: "(top {?R} {?isa} {?Dom} {?Ran}): {?a}∈{?Dom},{?b}∈{?Ran} → {?R}({?a},{?b})."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing.
The kernel's answer: Ann and Rex are a person and a pet.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (top))

(relation instance       T T           :why "{?3} is a {?4}")
(relation person-and-pet Person Pet    :why "a {?3} and a {?4}")
(instance Ann Person)
(instance Rex Pet)

;; R holds between every member of Dom and every member of Ran, by the
;; membership relation isa.
(top person-and-pet instance Person Pet)

(query :goal (person-and-pet ?p ?q) :goal-text "{?p} and {?q} are a person and a pet.")
(solve :no-search)
```

### (meet R S T)

Where R a b and S a b both hold, T a b holds. Its rule's own words: "(meet {?R1} {?R2} {?R3}): {?R1}({?a},{?b})∧{?R2}({?a},{?b}) → {?R3}({?a},{?b})."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing.
The kernel's answer: Ann is close to Bob.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (meet))

(relation lives-with Person Person :why "a {?3} lives with a {?4}")
(relation works-with Person Person :why "a {?3} works with a {?4}")
(relation is-close-to Person Person :why "a {?3} is close to a {?4}")
(lives-with Ann Bob)
(works-with Ann Bob)
(works-with Ann Cy)

;; Where both R a b and S a b hold, T a b.
(meet lives-with works-with is-close-to)

(query :goal (is-close-to Ann ?b) :goal-text "Ann is close to {?b}.")
(solve :no-search)
```

### (functional R)

A check: R a b and R a c, b and c two things, is a contradiction. Its rule's own words: "{?R} not functional: {?a} has both {?b} and {?c}."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing; it kills a state that breaks it.
The kernel's answer: Ann sits in the Front seat.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (functional))

(relation sits-in Person Seat :why "a {?3} sits in a {?4}")
(sits-in Ann Front)

;; A check: R a b and R a c with b and c two things is a contradiction, so a
;; state that supposes Ann sits in a second seat is dead.
(functional sits-in)

(open (sits-in Ann Back))

(query :goal (sits-in Ann ?s) :goal-text "Ann sits in the {?s} seat.")
```

### (injective R)

A check: R a c and R b c, a and b two things, is a contradiction. Its rule's own words: "{?R} not injective: {?c} reached from both {?a} and {?b}."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing; it kills a state that breaks it.
The kernel's answer: Ann sits in the front seat.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (injective))

(relation sits-in Person Seat :why "a {?3} sits in a {?4}")
(sits-in Ann Front)

;; A check: R a c and R b c with a and b two things is a contradiction, so a
;; state that supposes Bob sits in Ann's seat is dead.
(injective sits-in)

(open (sits-in Bob Front))

(query :goal (sits-in ?p Front) :goal-text "{?p} sits in the front seat.")
```

### (total-owed R isa)

An obligation: every member of R's first label owes a fact of R with a member of its second label. Its rule's own words: "{?R} owes {?a} a {?B}."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: the obligation's candidates are the hypotheses: the program states no other mechanism.
The kernel's answer: Bob sits in the Back seat.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (total-owed functional injective))

(relation instance T T           :why "{?3} is a {?4}")
(relation sits-in  Person Seat   :why "a {?3} sits in a {?4}")
(instance Ann   Person)
(instance Bob   Person)
(instance Front Seat)
(instance Back  Seat)
(sits-in Ann Front)

;; An obligation: every member of the first label owes a fact of R with a
;; member of the second, by the membership relation isa. Its candidates are
;; the hypotheses, so the program states no other mechanism.
(total-owed sits-in instance)
(functional sits-in)
(injective  sits-in)

(query :goal (sits-in Bob ?s) :goal-text "Bob sits in the {?s} seat.")
```

## std.slots: things in places: one relation whose classes are the places, each holding one value of every kind; its two selecting facts are no module's names, so the import lists nothing

### (slot-partition R isa sub Super Index)

R is an equivalence whose classes are the places: each place holds exactly one member of every kind that is a sub of Super, and its member of the kind Index names it. Its rule's own words: "(slot-partition {?R} …) ⟹ locate / exclusion / occupancy / negative-propagation / elimination / fill / no-room / no-fill / owed-room / owed-fill activators."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: its obligations propose every placement as a hypothesis: the program states no other mechanism.
The kernel's answer: Bob lives in the Right house.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (symmetric includes top meet))
(import std.slots)

(relation lives-in       Person House    :why "a {?3} lives in a {?4}")
(relation same-house     Attribute Attribute :why "a {?3} and a {?4} are in the same house")
(relation person-and-house Person House  :why "a {?3} and a {?4}")
(relation instance       T T             :why "{?3} is a {?4}")
(relation type           T T             :why "{?3} is a kind of {?4}")
(type Attribute T)
(type House     Attribute)
(type Person    Attribute)
(instance Left  House)
(instance Right House)
(instance Ann   Person)
(instance Bob   Person)
(lives-in Ann Left)

;; R is an equivalence whose classes are the places: each holds exactly one
;; member of every kind sub Super, and its Index member names it. Its
;; obligations propose every placement as a hypothesis.
(includes lives-in same-house)
(symmetric same-house)
(slot-partition same-house instance type Attribute House)

;; Read back: a person lives in the house it shares a place with.
(top  person-and-house instance Person House)
(meet same-house person-and-house lives-in)

(query :goal (lives-in Bob ?h) :goal-text "Bob lives in the {?h} house.")
```

### (slot-spatial R S isa Index)

S holds between two values exactly where it holds between their places, the places being the members of Index. Its rule's own words: "(slot-spatial {?R} {?S} …) ⟹ the eight directional propagation / pruning / endpoint activators."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing beside slot-partition's obligations.
The kernel's answer: Ann lives in the Right house.

```ein
(import std.algebra :symbols (symmetric includes top meet))
(import std.slots)

(relation lives-in       Person House    :why "a {?3} lives in a {?4}")
(relation is-left-of     Attribute Attribute :why "a {?3} is left of a {?4}")
(relation lives-left-of  Person Person   :why "a {?3} lives left of a {?4}")
(relation same-house     Attribute Attribute :why "a {?3} and a {?4} are in the same house")
(relation person-and-house Person House  :why "a {?3} and a {?4}")
(relation instance       T T             :why "{?3} is a {?4}")
(relation type           T T             :why "{?3} is a kind of {?4}")
(type Attribute T)
(type House     Attribute)
(type Person    Attribute)
(instance Left  House)
(instance Right House)
(instance Ann   Person)
(instance Bob   Person)
(is-left-of Left Right)
(lives-left-of Bob Ann)

(includes lives-in same-house)
(includes lives-left-of is-left-of)
(symmetric same-house)
(slot-partition same-house instance type Attribute House)
;; S holds between two values exactly where it holds between their places.
(slot-spatial same-house is-left-of instance House)

(top  person-and-house instance Person House)
(meet same-house person-and-house lives-in)

(query :goal (lives-in Ann ?h) :goal-text "Ann lives in the {?h} house.")
```

## std.bijection: a relation one to one between the members of its two labels; its selecting facts are no module's names, so the import lists nothing

### (bijective R) and (bijection-hierarchy isa)

R is one to one between the members of its two labels: each member of the first has exactly one of the second, and each of the second exactly one of the first. Its rule's own words: "(bijective {?R}) + hierarchy {?isa} ⟹ totality / obligations / elimination / negative-completion activators."
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: its obligations propose the facts of R as hypotheses: the program states no other mechanism.
The kernel's answer: Cy wears Blue.

```ein
(import std.bijection)

(relation instance T T         :why "{?3} is a {?4}")
(relation wears    Person Color :why "a {?3} wears {?4}")
(instance Ann  Person)
(instance Bob  Person)
(instance Cy   Person)
(instance Red   Color)
(instance Green Color)
(instance Blue  Color)
(wears Ann Red)
(not (wears Bob Blue))

;; R is one to one between its two labels: each member of the first has
;; exactly one of the second and each of the second exactly one of the first.
;; Its obligations propose the facts of R as hypotheses.
(bijective wears)
(bijection-hierarchy instance)

(query :goal (wears Cy ?c) :goal-text "Cy wears {?c}.")
```

## std.contradiction: a fact beside its stored negation is a contradiction; std.algebra, std.slots and std.bijection import it already

### (import std.contradiction)

A fact beside its stored negation is a contradiction, for every relation the program declares.
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing.
The kernel's answer: the statements contradict each other.

```ein
(import std.contradiction)

(relation sits-in Person Seat :why "a {?3} sits in a {?4}")
(sits-in Ann Back)

;; With the module imported, a fact beside its stored negation is a
;; contradiction, for every relation the program declares: here root holds
;; both, and root is dead.
(not (sits-in Ann Back))

(query :goal (sits-in Ann ?s) :goal-text "Ann sits in the {?s} seat.")
(solve :no-search)
```

## std.math.decimal-arith: the calculator: a natural number is a numeral, z is zero, (n z 5) is 5, (n (n z 1) 2) is 12; a rule states a request and reads its result back; the three imports always go together

### (add X Y), answered by (sum-of X Y S)

S is X plus Y.
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing; the entry states (solve :no-search).
The kernel's answer: They have (n (n z 1) 2) apples together.

```ein
(import std.math.decimal-arith :symbols (add sum-of))
(import std.math.decimal :symbols (n))
(import std.math.arith :symbols (z))

(relation has     Person Number :why "a {?3} has a {?4} of apples")
(relation have-together Number  :why "they have {?3} apples together")
(has Ann (n z 5))
(has Bob (n z 7))

;; A request (add X Y) is answered by (sum-of X Y S): a rule states the
;; request and another reads the result back.
(rule together-asks-the-sum ()
  :match  (and (has Ann ?a) (has Bob ?b))
  :assert (add ?a ?b)
  :why    "Ann has {?a} and Bob {?b}: ask {?a} plus {?b}")
(rule together-is-the-sum ()
  :match  (and (has Ann ?a) (has Bob ?b) (sum-of ?a ?b ?s))
  :assert (have-together ?s)
  :why    "{?a} and {?b} make {?s}")

(query :goal (have-together ?s) :goal-text "They have {?s} apples together.")
(solve :no-search)
```

### (sub X Y), answered by (diff-of X Y D R)

D is X less Y where X is not the smaller, else z; R is Y less X where X is the smaller, else z.
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing; the entry states (solve :no-search).
The kernel's answer: Ann has (n z 7) more apples.

```ein
(import std.math.decimal-arith :symbols (sub diff-of))
(import std.math.decimal :symbols (n))
(import std.math.arith :symbols (z))

(relation has      Person Number :why "a {?3} has a {?4} of apples")
(relation has-more Person Number :why "a {?3} has a {?4} more")
(has Ann (n (n z 1) 2))
(has Bob (n z 5))

;; (sub X Y) is answered by (diff-of X Y D R): D is X less Y where X is not
;; the smaller, else z; R is Y less X where X is the smaller, else z.
(rule more-asks-the-difference ()
  :match  (and (has Ann ?a) (has Bob ?b))
  :assert (sub ?a ?b)
  :why    "Ann has {?a} and Bob {?b}: ask {?a} less {?b}")
(rule more-is-the-difference ()
  :match  (and (has Ann ?a) (has Bob ?b) (diff-of ?a ?b ?d z))
  :assert (has-more Ann ?d)
  :why    "{?a} less {?b} is {?d}")

(query :goal (has-more Ann ?d) :goal-text "Ann has {?d} more apples.")
(solve :no-search)
```

### (mul X Y), answered by (product-of X Y P)

P is X times Y.
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing; the entry states (solve :no-search).
The kernel's answer: The hall has (n (n z 2) 4) seats.

```ein
(import std.math.decimal-arith :symbols (mul product-of))
(import std.math.decimal :symbols (n))
(import std.math.arith :symbols (z))

(relation has-rows  Room Number :why "a {?3} has {?4} rows")
(relation row-seats Room Number :why "a row of a {?3} has {?4} seats")
(relation seats     Room Number :why "a {?3} has {?4} seats")
(has-rows  Hall (n z 4))
(row-seats Hall (n z 6))

;; (mul X Y) is answered by (product-of X Y P).
(rule seats-asks-the-product ()
  :match  (and (has-rows ?r ?k) (row-seats ?r ?m))
  :assert (mul ?k ?m)
  :why    "{?r} has {?k} rows of {?m}: ask {?k} times {?m}")
(rule seats-is-the-product ()
  :match  (and (has-rows ?r ?k) (row-seats ?r ?m) (product-of ?k ?m ?p))
  :assert (seats ?r ?p)
  :why    "{?k} rows of {?m} are {?p} seats")

(query :goal (seats Hall ?p) :goal-text "The hall has {?p} seats.")
(solve :no-search)
```

### (div X Y), answered by (quot-of X Y Q R)

Q is X divided by Y and R what is left.
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing; the entry states (solve :no-search).
The kernel's answer: Each friend gets (n z 3) sweets and (n z 2) are left.

```ein
(import std.math.decimal-arith :symbols (div quot-of))
(import std.math.decimal :symbols (n))
(import std.math.arith :symbols (z))

(relation has      Person Number :why "a {?3} has {?4} sweets")
(relation shared-among Person Number :why "a {?3} shares among {?4} friends")
(relation each-gets Number Number  :why "each friend gets {?3} and {?4} are left")
(has Ann (n (n z 1) 7))
(shared-among Ann (n z 5))

;; (div X Y) is answered by (quot-of X Y Q R): Q the quotient, R the rest.
(rule sharing-asks-the-quotient ()
  :match  (and (has Ann ?a) (shared-among Ann ?k))
  :assert (div ?a ?k)
  :why    "Ann shares {?a} among {?k}: ask {?a} divided by {?k}")
(rule sharing-is-the-quotient ()
  :match  (and (has Ann ?a) (shared-among Ann ?k) (quot-of ?a ?k ?q ?r))
  :assert (each-gets ?q ?r)
  :why    "{?a} among {?k} is {?q} each and {?r} left")

(query :goal (each-gets ?q ?r) :goal-text "Each friend gets {?q} sweets and {?r} are left.")
(solve :no-search)
```

### (cmp X Y), answered by (cmp-of X Y O)

O is lt, eq or gt as X is less than, equal to or more than Y.
What it gives the hypothesis mechanism: nothing; the entry states (solve :no-search).
The kernel's answer: Ann is older than Bob.

```ein
(import std.math.decimal-arith :symbols (cmp cmp-of))
(import std.math.decimal :symbols (n))
(import std.math.arith :symbols (z))

(relation is-years-old Person Number :why "a {?3} is {?4} years old")
(relation is-older-than Person Person :why "a {?3} is older than a {?4}")
(is-years-old Ann (n z 9))
(is-years-old Bob (n z 7))

;; (cmp X Y) is answered by (cmp-of X Y O), O one of lt, eq and gt.
(rule older-asks-the-comparison ()
  :match  (and (is-years-old ?a ?x) (is-years-old ?b ?y) (neq ?a ?b))
  :assert (cmp ?x ?y)
  :why    "{?a} is {?x} and {?b} is {?y}: compare them")
(rule older-is-greater ()
  :match  (and (is-years-old ?a ?x) (is-years-old ?b ?y) (cmp-of ?x ?y gt))
  :assert (is-older-than ?a ?b)
  :why    "{?x} is more than {?y}, so {?a} is older than {?b}")

(query :goal (is-older-than ?a ?b) :goal-text "{?a} is older than {?b}.")
(solve :no-search)
```
